  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "I submitted **part-based** with confidence 0.88. The deciding result is that a task built only from rules the learner already knew was learned faster than tasks with new rules, even though its responses matched no trained task. I used 42 of the 60 budget units.\n\n**Design:** I trained the learner on two tasks, L1-S1-M1 and L4-S4-M4. I then used the free `compare` command to pick test tasks where the two hypotheses predict opposite orderings. \"Similarity\" below is the highest match with either trained task.\n\n| Test task | New rules | Similarity | Mean examples to criterion |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L4-S1-M1 | 0 | 0.0 | 20 (n=12) |\n| L1-S1-M4, L4-S4-M1 | 0 | 0.375 | 18 (n=7) |\n| L1-S1-M3 | 1 (M3) | 0.75 | 22 (n=3) |\n| L2-S3-M3 | 3 | 0.5625 | 33 (n=12) |\n| L2-S2-M2 | 3 | 0.19 | 35 (n=8) |\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- If the learner were holistic, L4-S1-M1 should have been the hardest task, since it gives a different answer from both trained tasks on every input. It was among the easiest.\n- The two tasks with three new rules took about the same number of examples, even though one was much more similar to the trained tasks. Similarity made no visible difference.\n- Cost rose with the number of new rules: about 19 for none, 22 for one, about 34 for three. That is the part-based pattern.\n\n**Caveats:** The tests are very noisy. Single runs of the same task ranged from 1 to 47 examples. The main gap (about 19 vs 34) is roughly 2.5 to 3 standard errors, which is why I put confidence at 0.88 rather than higher. The one-new-rule result is based on only three runs.",
